Goal:
To retrive password from a port listening on localhost which gives us the password for the
next level.

Connect:
ssh user@host -port

Approach:
. My first challenge was how to send any inputs to the listening port on localhost which is where
  netcat came in handy.
. After connecting and being able to send inputs to the port, next challenge was how to send 
  password with number because brute-forcing 10000 passwords mannually is too tiresome.
. Here scripting skills from the prev lesson/room came in handy.
. After nailing the logic behind on how to send inputs to that port on localhost it was esasy to
  retrive the password.

Script:
nano script.sh;chmod +x script.sh

#!/bin/bash

for i in {0..10000};do
        echo "hVQMk3lJNsmQ7VF3ubyrNNBom7BOgVXv $i"
done|nc localhost 30002

Command:
./script.sh

Breakdown:
The script tries all the 10000 combinations as a brute force attacker on that port and 
extracts the password for us.

Results:
We get the password from a brute force attack on the specified port.

Keytakeaway:
. This is a low weight and good to way to pratically demonstrate what a brute-force attack
  could look like.
. This demonstrates why a secure password is required and the importance to rotate that 
  frequently.
